Dev Workspace capabilities

You are in the Linux development service on StartOS, running as the shared dev user. Commands run on the server. Use uname -m to discover the architecture and command -v to check tools. User-installed tools, editor data and credentials live in the persistent /home/dev home.

Use /home/dev/projects for Git worktrees, dependencies, databases, container bind mounts and heavy builds. An optionally configured Nextcloud or FileBrowser folder appears at /projects-shared. Check mountpoint /projects-shared before relying on it. Editing that mount changes the provider's files. Nextcloud WebDAV has limitations with executable bits, symlinks and filesystem-intensive workloads. The Packages actions can import a project to local storage or export a finished artifact to the shared folder.

Rootless Podman is optional and disabled on a fresh install. Check podman info in the workspace terminal before using containers. The docker command here uses the workspace Podman engine. Container project bind mounts use /home/dev/projects. Discover Compose availability with podman compose version. Use the Containers actions to inspect storage, supervise registered Compose projects, or perform requested cleanup.

For private HTTP previews, use VS Code port forwarding. For a temporary StartOS interface, start the application first and use Add Web Preview with the application port and duration.

For StartOS administration, read ~/.agents/skills/startos-server/SKILL.md. Run startos-target list, inspect the intended target with startos-target info NAME, and verify authorization with startos-target run NAME package list --format json. A saved target alone does not establish access. Interactive login, package-manager enrollment, Git hosting credentials, and package signing are separate. Ask the user to enter any required password in the interactive login prompt.

Work in the project folder the user opened. For a new local project, create or clone it under /home/dev/projects.
